23:03:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re definitely not alone in thinking that—it’s a very common mental model! But in reality, **developers don’t “send” the update directly to your device** like a text message or a broadcast. Here’s why that direct-push idea doesn’t work, and what actually happens instead: --- ### Why a direct “send to everyone” doesn’t happen 1. **The developer doesn’t have a master list of every device.** They don’t know your IP address, your device ID, or when you’ll be online. There’s no giant address book where they can just hit “send to all.” 2. **Most devices are behind firewalls/NATs** and can’t receive unsolicited incoming connections from the internet—your home router or mobile carrier would block them. So a server can’t suddenly push a file to your phone while you’re just sitting there. 3. **The scale is impossible.** Even if they tried, sending a 500 MB update to 100 million devices at the same moment would instantly melt any single server. You need a distributed system that spreads the load. 4. **Devices are often offline.** When an update is released, millions of phones, consoles, or PCs are turned off or not connected. A push would miss them entirely. --- ### What really happens: Your device “pulls” the update when it’s ready The process is **initiated by your device**, not by the developer’s server. It works more like checking your mailbox than receiving a phone call: - Your device runs a background service (Windows Update, Google Play, Steam, your console’s OS, etc.) that **periodically asks the vendor’s server**: “Hey, is there anything new for me?” - The server just answers “Yes, here’s what you need” or “No.” It doesn’t reach out first. - If an update is available, your device **downloads** it from a content delivery network (CDN), a huge network of copycat servers located around the globe, so the files come from a location near you—not from the developer’s single computer. --- ### Even when you get a “notification” that an update is ready, that’s still a pull For mobile apps or game launchers, you sometimes see a popup: “A new update is available!” That might feel like a push, but it’s not the update file being pushed. It’s usually just a tiny **push notification** (a few bytes of text) saying “there’s an update,” which then **triggers your device to go pull the actual data**. The heavy lifting—the multi‑gigabyte download—is always pulled. The only common exception is some enterprise IT systems where a company’s own server can force‑install updates on devices it manages, but even then, the device is configured to check in frequently and obey the command; the files are still pulled by the device. --- ### So the simplified mental model is: - ❌ **Developer → directly sends update to each device** (nope) - ✅ **Your device → regularly asks a server → server says yes → device downloads from a nearby CDN server** (yes) It’s like you going to the app store and hitting “Update All” instead of the developer mailing you a CD. The system is designed that way for security, scalability, and reliability.
