23:03:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a completely fair assumption—it really *feels* like the update is being pushed to you, especially when you wake up to a notification that says “Update installed automatically.” But the “push” feeling is a clever illusion created by how often and how aggressively your device checks for updates. The actual mechanism is still a **pull**, just made to look seamless. Let me break down why it feels like a direct push, but isn’t: --- ### The illusion of “automatic push” 1. **Your device is configured to check *very frequently*.** - On iOS, the App Store and system updater check at regular intervals, sometimes as often as every few minutes for app updates. - Android does the same via Google Play Services, and game consoles often check while in “rest mode” or immediately upon waking. - Windows Update can be set to check every day, or even more often if you enable “optional updates” delivery. Because the check is so frequent, the moment a developer flips the switch on the server, your device discovers the update within a very short window. To you, that seems instantaneous—like a push. 2. **The download starts in the background without you asking.** Many modern systems are set to “Download updates automatically.” So after your device *pulls* the notification that an update exists, it immediately starts *pulling* the download package. You never had to click “Download.” That’s another layer that hides the pull. 3. **Installation can also be automatic.** For example, Android apps can auto-update over Wi‑Fi. Consoles can install game updates while in rest mode. Windows can install during off‑hours. The entire sequence—check, download, install—happens without your input, making it feel like the developer sent it directly. But at the network level, **none of these steps are initiated by the server**. The first packet always comes from your device asking, “Anything for me?” Only then does the server respond with the update data. --- ### A simple analogy: Your mailbox vs. a phone call - **A true push** would be like the developer calling your phone and handing you the update file without you even picking up. That’s not technically possible across the open internet for normal consumer devices. - **What actually happens** is more like you have a personal assistant (the update service) that goes to the post office every few minutes to check your P.O. box. If a package (update) is there, the assistant brings it back and unpacks it for you, sometimes while you’re asleep. It feels like the package arrived on its own, but your assistant made the trip. --- ### Even “push notifications” aren’t file pushes You might get a push notification saying “New update available.” That tiny alert is indeed a push (through services like Apple Push Notification Service or Firebase Cloud Messaging). But it’s just a trigger message—it tells your device to go pull the actual update. The multi‑gigabyte download is never pushed; that would cripple the notification service and your battery. --- ### So, to directly address your thought: “Aren’t updates automatically pushed to all devices once they’re released?” If we’re speaking technically, **no**, because the download is always a pull. But if we’re speaking from the user’s experience, **yes**—the magic of frequent checks and background installs makes it feel like an automatic push. The distinction matters for understanding why a single server doesn’t crash, and why you sometimes don’t get an update for a few days during staged rollouts (your device just didn’t pull it yet). In short: You’re right that the experience is “automatic,” but under the hood, your device is doing the legwork by constantly asking, “Is it my turn yet?”
