ercentage of compatible devices at first (say, 1%). They watch for crashes and bugs. Over a few days or weeks, that percentage slowly increases to 100%. - **Random or ID‑based selection**: Your device is assigned to a group. If you’re in the 1% bucket, your phone’s pull will be met with “Yes, here’s the update.” If you’re in the 99% bucket, the server will say “Not yet,” even though you’re both checking at the same time. That’s why two identical phones side‑by‑side might get the same update days apart. - **Check‑in stagger**: Even without phased rollout, every device doesn’t check at the exact same second. The update service has a random offset so they spread out throughout the hour or day. So no, it’s never a synchronized blast to all phones at once. --- ### A simple analogy to tie it all together Imagine a library that gets a new bestseller. Instead of mailing a copy to every resident at the same moment, the library simply puts the book on the shelf. You, the reader, have a habit of walking to the library every night while you’re asleep (your phone checks on Wi‑Fi). The librarian (the CDN) sees you and says, “Here’s the book you’ve been waiting for,” and you take it home. Because your trip is automatic and happens when you’re not paying attention, it *feels* like the book magically appeared on your nightstand—but it didn’t; you just fetched it without conscious effort. --- ### The bottom line Your phone’s excellent automatic behavior makes it **feel** like a direct push, and for all practical purposes, it’s as convenient as one. But if the update were truly a simultaneous broadcast, the internet’s infrastructure would break every release day. Instead, each device silently, patiently, and secretly *pulls* the update when its turn comes and the conditions are right. That’s the real magic behind the seamless experience.
